.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt SUDO 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sudo
.Nd run a command as root if the real user is permitted
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar COMMAND
.Op Ar ARG ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the reference implementation of a coursework exercise.
It is installed set-user-ID root only in the test image, where
.Nm sudoprobe
tests it.
The os-real image provides
.Xr su 1
and leaves
.Nm
for the student to write.
.Pp
.Nm
reads
.Pa /etc/sudoers ,
which lists permitted numeric user IDs, one per line.
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.Ql #
are ignored.
The check uses the caller's real user ID, which the caller cannot forge.
A missing or unreadable file permits no one.
Lines longer than 127 bytes before the newline are rejected in full;
a continuation of a comment cannot authorize a user.
.Pp
For a permitted caller,
.Nm
closes descriptors 3 through 1023 before changing identity,
assumes full root identity through its saved ID, and executes
.Ar COMMAND
by its explicit path, passing the remaining arguments to it.
The command receives a clean environment containing only
.Ev PATH
and
.Ev HOME .
Other callers are refused.
.Pp
Per-command rules, password prompting, and terminal handling are left
for the exercise.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/sudoers
.It Pa /etc/sudoers
Numeric user IDs permitted to run commands as root.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Without a command,
.Nm
prints:
.Dl usage: sudo <command> [args...]
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr passwd 5
